#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171644 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/id #uuid "8ed57b1f-0082-5281-b061-ff2020ed7d66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12364" :question/problem "The fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=e^{x/2}$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in [0,2]$ is given by\n$\\big\\langle \\text{_______}\\,, \\:-e^{x/2} \\cos\\theta, \\: -e^{x/2}\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle$.\n\nWhat is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\ny=e^{x/2} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in [0,2]$. The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr = ⟨x, f(x)\\cos θ, f(x)\\sin θ⟩, (x, θ) ∈ D\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nf(x)=e^{x/2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi \\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{x}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x}, \\frac{∂y}{∂x}, \\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩ = ⟨1, f^{′}\\cos θ, f^{′}\\sin θ⟩ \\\\\nr_{θ}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂θ}, \\frac{∂y}{∂θ}, \\frac{∂z}{∂θ}⟩ = ⟨0,-f\\sin θ, f\\cos θ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{θ}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & f^{′}\\cos θ & f^{′}\\sin θ \\\\ 0 & -f\\sin θ & f\\cos θ\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}f^{′}\\cos θ & f^{′}\\sin θ \\\\ -f\\sin θ & f\\cos θ\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & f^{′}\\sin θ \\\\ 0 & f\\cos θ\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}1 & f^{′}\\cos θ \\\\ 0 & -f\\sin θ\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ff^{′}\\cos^{2} θ + ff^{′}\\sin^{2} θ,-f\\cos θ,-f\\sin θ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ff^{′},-f\\cos θ,-f\\sin θ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf=e^{x/2}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nf' = \\dfrac12 e^{x/2}\n$$\n\nSo, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\nr_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{θ}^{′} = ⟨\\frac{1}{2}e^{x},-e^{x/2}\\cos θ,-e^{x/2}\\sin θ⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $1$st component of the product is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}e^x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171645 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/id #uuid "a2ff0c76-0d26-51a2-a2fb-498ff5f85559" :question/math-academy-id "e-12365" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to find an integral expression for the surface area generated when the curve $x=\\sin z$ is rotated by $2\\pi$ radians about the $z$-axis for $z \\in [0, \\pi]$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\nx=\\sin z \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $z \\in [0, \\pi]$. The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr = ⟨f(z)\\cos θ, f(z)\\sin θ, z⟩, (z, θ) ∈ D\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nf(z)=\\sin z\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(z,\\theta) \\,: \\, 0 \\leq z \\leq \\pi, \\, 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{z}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z}, \\frac{∂y}{∂z}, \\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩ = ⟨f^{′}\\cos θ, f^{′}\\sin θ, 1⟩ \\\\\nr_{θ}^{′} &= ⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂θ}, \\frac{∂y}{∂θ}, \\frac{∂z}{∂θ}⟩ = ⟨ - f\\sin θ, f\\cos θ, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_{z}^{′} \\cdot r_{θ}^{′} &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ f^{′}\\cos θ & f^{′}\\sin θ & 1 \\\\ -f\\sin θ & f\\cos θ & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= ⟨\\begin{vmatrix}f^{′}\\sin θ & 1 \\\\ f\\cos θ & 0\\end{vmatrix}, -\\begin{vmatrix}f^{′}\\cos θ & 1 \\\\ -f\\sin θ & 0\\end{vmatrix},\\begin{vmatrix}f^{′}\\cos θ & f^{′}\\sin θ \\\\ -f\\sin θ & f\\cos θ\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - f\\cos θ,-f\\sin θ, f^{′}f\\cos^{2} θ + f^{′}f\\sin^{2} θ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - f\\cos θ,-f\\sin θ, f^{′}f⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r_{z}^{′} \\cdot r_{θ}^{′}‖ &= \\sqrt{(-f\\cos θ)^{2} + (-f\\sin θ)^{2} + (f^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{f^{2}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ) + f^{2}(f^{′})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{f^{2} + f^{2}(f^{′})^{2}} \\\\\n&= f\\sqrt{1 + (f^{′})^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∬_(S)dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)‖r_{z}^{′} \\cdot r_{θ}^{′}‖dzdθ \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)f\\sqrt{1 + (f^{′})^{2}}dzdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}f\\sqrt{1 + (f^{′})^{2}}∫_{0}^{2π}dθdz \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}f\\sqrt{1 + (f^{′})^{2}} \\cdot θ \\mid _{0}^{2π}dz \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}f\\sqrt{1 + (f^{′})^{2}}dz\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(z)=\\sin z\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nf'(z) = \\cos z\n$$\n\nSo, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2π∫_{0}^{π}f\\sqrt{1 + (f^{′})^{2}}dz &= 2π∫_{0}^{π}\\sin z\\sqrt{1 + (\\cos z)^{2}}dz \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}\\sin z\\sqrt{1 + \\cos^{2} z}dz\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171794 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ac4af78-af9b-5d48-a5d7-7d47ad92e348" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the x-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "1d32df15-74ee-5a3d-bc33-3d6452700e84" :question/math-academy-id "e-8879" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=x^2$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x \\in \\left[0, 1\\right]$. What is $\\mathbf{r}$?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3372/Source/Images/34f3efad20bb1b36ba124e1cf3ca11d1.png)\n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in[a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$[MATH: r = ⟨x, f(x)\\cos θ, f(x)\\sin θ⟩, x ∈ [a, b], θ ∈ [0, 2π).]$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\ny=f(x) = x^2 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\nx \\in \\left[0,1\\right]\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$[MATH: \\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta) = \\big\\langle x, \\: x^2 \\cos\\theta, \\: x^2\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad x \\in \\left[0, 1\\right], \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171795 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the y-Axis or z-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "6ab25451-ad5c-5437-b4ba-046b0bd30fe6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8880" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(y,\\theta)$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=1-\\cos{y}$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis for $y \\in [0, \\pi]$. What is $\\mathbf{r}$?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3372/Source/Images/e415b25ee1a73d8953b38a5cec1ac802.png)\n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$[MATH: r = ⟨f(y)\\cos θ, y, f(y)\\sin θ⟩, y ∈ [a, b], θ = [0, 2π).]$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nx=f(y) = 1-\\cos{y} \\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor all $y \\in [0, \\pi]$.\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$[MATH: \\mathbf{r}(y,\\theta) = \\big\\langle (1-\\cos{y}) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: (1-\\cos{y}) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y \\in[0, \\pi], \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdaf06ad-8a5e-55d8-857e-f7335543f3c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + 4y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168188} {:answer/id #uuid "d04a8a98-3f2a-5ac2-b54b-cdb02e7eb5c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1 + 4y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168189} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f4ca0c-8d40-5d35-9bc7-f5dd04d80227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168190} {:answer/id #uuid "02783d3a-d9a1-5452-822d-966d5e0130ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + 4y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168191} {:answer/id #uuid "6841d782-b072-579a-b093-77118b70440e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdaf06ad-8a5e-55d8-857e-f7335543f3c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-1}^{1}(1 + y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + 4y^{2}}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168188} :answer-field/id #uuid "86c16368-7829-5fe0-a98e-4f96d9855360" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168187}] :question/id #uuid "bba1677a-6311-54bf-bad0-6a9e581cdd7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-106058" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x = 1 + y^{2}$ is rotated by $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis for $y ∈ [- 1, 1]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82a54cc3-ca46-5b66-b02e-cc4ec1cfee8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin y\\cos^{3} y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169070} {:answer/id #uuid "c20c86d9-0959-5f44-9492-658b5eb50883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin 2y\\cos y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169071} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ebad9e-77c4-5c15-9810-77a468ff78e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin y\\cos^{2} y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169072} {:answer/id #uuid "ff879225-7b68-56ed-a06b-ae7dcda19dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin 2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169073} {:answer/id #uuid "2214456d-c3d9-5c9c-a6a5-b4cbab39d0dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sin^{2} 2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82a54cc3-ca46-5b66-b02e-cc4ec1cfee8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin y\\cos^{3} y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169070} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2295a86-f4f1-52eb-b137-a20b7ef4a93c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169069}] :question/id #uuid "5660a1ea-1c96-5f39-8bde-a992c1f8c03a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161069" :question/problem "$⟨\\cos^{2} y\\cos θ, _______, \\cos^{2} y\\sin θ⟩$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x = \\cos^{2} y$ by $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis for $y ∈ [- \\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2}]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd component of the product?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ae28bc2-f9a0-5d59-bace-6845445f5266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{3}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169076} {:answer/id #uuid "e84bd21c-42f3-570e-9500-c24f14ce38e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π∫_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{3}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169077} {:answer/id #uuid "ea344160-f2e6-5a60-b449-05b530fdcad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 16x^{6}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169078} {:answer/id #uuid "00df6180-a1be-57fa-83f1-d9db68dffca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π∫_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 16x^{6}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169079} {:answer/id #uuid "297c0a53-60c2-545d-a4e9-631696e3a55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 4x^{6}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea344160-f2e6-5a60-b449-05b530fdcad0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{-2}^{2}x^{4}\\sqrt{1 + 16x^{6}}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169078} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd1a3809-886e-5fc6-b6f6-9ef2ef43a75a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169075}] :question/id #uuid "929a0232-4773-59cb-9c62-800f13322539" :question/math-academy-id "q-161095" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y = x^{4}$ is rotated by $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis for $x ∈ [- 2, 2]$."}]}
